.TH FAULTPROBE 1
.SH NAME
faultprobe \- fault on purpose, to calibrate a harness
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B faultprobe
[
.B -b
]
[
.B -e
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Faultprobe
fails, deliberately, in one of three ways: dereferencing nil by default,
indexing past the end of an array with
.BR -b ,
or raising an exception nobody handles with
.BR -e .
.PP
It exists because a harness that watches for failures is only as good as its
ability to see one, and in this tree that is not obvious.
.IR Wm (1)
intercepts Limbo's
.B print
into its own Log window, so a good deal of what a program says while
.I wm
is running never reaches whatever started it.
A run reporting no faults may mean there were none, or may mean the watcher was
reading the wrong stream, and nothing in the log tells the two apart.
.PP
So run this under the harness in question and see whether the harness notices.
If it does not, it cannot be trusted to notice a real one.
.SH "WHAT IT FOUND IMMEDIATELY"
The three failures do not look alike from outside, which is the whole point.
Under
.IR wm (1):
.IP
.EX
nil dereference   disfault: dereference of nil, and a backtrace
array bounds      sh: /dis/faultprobe.dis: exception: array bounds error
unhandled raise   sh: ...: exception: <whatever was raised>
.EE
.PP
A watcher looking for
.BR broken ,
.BR faults ,
.B panic
or
.B abort
\(em which is what the resize stress harness was using \(em sees the first and
misses the other two entirely.
It had been reporting no faults across a batch of runs, and that was worth
exactly nothing until this said so.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/faultprobe.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wmtest (1),
.IR clicktest (1),
.IR emu (1)
.SH BUGS
Three ways to fail, out of many.
A process that hangs, one that exits quietly with the wrong answer, and one
that corrupts something and carries on are all failures this cannot imitate,
and a harness that catches all three of these still catches none of those.
.PP
It says nothing about
.I where
output goes, only whether it arrives.
Working out that the Log window was swallowing something takes reading
.IR wm (1),
not running this.
